1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,520
This is Doing and Feeling, Episode 3, Intelligence Formally.

2
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:10,840
In 2006, the computer scientist Marcus Hutter put up prize money for an odd challenge.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:15,500
Compress 100 megabytes of English Wikipedia as small as possible.

4
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:23,460
The program that decompresses it counts toward the total size, so you cannot cheat by hiding the text inside the decompressor.

5
00:00:23,460 --> 00:00:35,760
Since 2020, the challenge has used a full gigabyte, with a prize fund of up to half a million euros, and the record fell three times in 2026, most recently in July.

6
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:39,440
Why would zipping Wikipedia have anything to do with intelligence?

7
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:49,940
The answer connects information theory, the foundations of statistics, a mathematical definition of intelligence, and the training objective of every large language model.

8
00:00:50,670 --> 00:00:54,380
Last episode, intelligence took many forms in many bodies.

9
00:00:54,840 --> 00:01:00,460
This one asks whether there's a definition that does not depend on any species, body, or test.

10
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:01,880
Here's the argument.

11
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:07,920
The best formal definitions tie intelligence to prediction and compression across many environments.

12
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:14,380
But every definition builds in assumptions about what counts as simple or about which tasks matter,

13
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,540
and no learner is best at everything.

14
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:20,380
That is why measuring machine intelligence is so contested,

15
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:24,580
and why a score can change dramatically depending on how a model is run.

16
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:27,200
Start with Alan Turing.

17
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:30,920
In 1950, he proposed replacing the question,

18
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:36,040
can machines think, which he considered too meaningless to deserve discussion, with a game.

19
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:40,900
An interrogator converses by text with a hidden human and a hidden machine.

20
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:44,360
If the interrogator cannot reliably tell which is which,

21
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:47,420
the machine has done everything we could reasonably ask.

22
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:51,120
The imitation game is operational, which is its virtue.

23
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:54,860
It is also a test of human likeness, which is its weakness.

24
00:01:55,200 --> 00:02:01,060
In 2025, Cameron Jones and Benjamin Burgin ran a pre-registered version

25
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:03,500
with Turing's original three-party design.

26
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:07,980
Judges chatted for five minutes with a human and a machine at the same time,

27
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:09,860
then picked which one was human.

28
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:14,700
GPT-4.5, prompted to adopt a particular human-like persona,

29
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:20,220
was judged to be the human 73% of the time, more often than the real humans were.

30
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:25,460
A meta model given the same persona prompt was picked 56% of the time.

31
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:33,360
Baseline systems, GPT-4.0 and the 1960s chatbot Eliza, were picked only about a fifth of the time.

32
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:38,860
So both of the systems that passed were playing a character someone had written for them.

33
00:02:39,860 --> 00:02:44,900
lesson. What the test measured was how well a system could play a particular kind of human.

34
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:50,340
That's a real capability, and a socially important one, given what it implies about

35
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:54,580
who you might be talking to online. But it is not a definition of intelligence.

36
00:02:55,700 --> 00:03:01,220
The deeper story starts with Claude Shannon. In his 1948 theory of communication,

37
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:07,780
information is surprise. An event with probability p carries minus log 2 of p bits.

38
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:12,940
In a 1951 paper, Shannon estimated the information content of English

39
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:17,480
by asking people to guess the next letter of a text, one letter at a time.

40
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:22,500
People were good at it because they understood the language and the world the text described.

41
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:27,020
Shannon was using people's knowledge of the language as a measuring instrument.

42
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:31,260
The better they guessed, the less information each new letter carried.

43
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:32,680
Here's the key link.

44
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:36,280
A technique called arithmetic coding can encode a symbol

45
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:43,580
p using very nearly minus log 2 of p bits. So the better your model predicts the next symbol,

46
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:49,660
the fewer bits you need to encode it. Prediction and compression are the same problem. Train a

47
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:54,580
model to minimize prediction error, measured as cross-entropy, and you are literally training

48
00:03:54,580 --> 00:04:00,720
it to compress. Which is why language model loss is reported in bits or Nats per token.

49
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:07,040
Per training is compression with extra steps. And researchers at DeepMind made that literal

50
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:15,000
paper titled Language Modeling is Compression. They used a large model trained mostly on text

51
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,640
as a general-purpose compressor, and it squeezed image patches and audio smaller than the standard

52
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:27,620
specialized formats, PNG, and FLAC. The caveat matters. Count the size of the model itself,

53
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:33,820
and it loses badly because the model is enormous. But the result shows how much general structure

54
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:40,280
a good predictor absorbs. If good prediction means good compression, what is the best possible

55
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:47,320
predictor? In the 1960s, Ray Solomonoff gave a remarkable answer. Consider every computer program

56
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:52,340
that could have produced the data you've seen so far. Weight each program by two to the minus

57
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:58,320
its length in bits so that shorter programs count for much more. Then predict the next observation

58
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:01,880
by averaging over what all those programs would output next.

59
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:04,800
This is Occam's razor made mathematical.

60
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:08,700
The length of the shortest program that produces a given string

61
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:11,000
is called its Kalmogorov complexity,

62
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:15,340
after Andrei Kalmogorov, who developed the idea independently,

63
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:17,220
as did Gregory Chaitin.

64
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:21,540
Solomanov showed that if the data come from any computable process at all,

65
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:25,920
his predictor's total error is bounded by the complexity of that process.

66
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:28,240
It learns any computable world,

67
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:31,920
and no computable predictor can beat it by more than a constant.

68
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:34,600
The catch is that it is uncomputable.

69
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:38,340
Evaluating it would require knowing which programs ever halt,

70
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:40,240
which is impossible in general.

71
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:43,440
So it is a gold standard, not an algorithm.

72
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:47,800
But it gives a precise sense in which intelligence and compression are linked.

73
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:52,540
The best predictor is the one that finds the shortest explanation of the data.

74
00:05:53,440 --> 00:06:02,500
In 2007, Shane Legge, who would later co-found DeepMind, and Marcus Hutter used this machinery to define intelligence itself.

75
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:11,660
They first gathered around 70 informal definitions from psychologists, AI researchers, and dictionaries, and distilled their common core.

76
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:17,240
Intelligence measures an agent's ability to achieve goals in a wide range of environments.

77
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:19,080
Then they formalized it.

78
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:22,700
Imagine every computable environment an agent could face,

79
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:25,720
each one providing observations and rewards.

80
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:29,120
Measure the reward the agent can expect to earn in each.

81
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:31,760
Then take a weighted sum over all of them,

82
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:36,100
weighting each environment by 2 to the minus its Kalmogorov complexity

83
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:38,740
so that simpler environments count more.

84
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:41,840
That sum is the agent's universal intelligence.

85
00:06:42,580 --> 00:06:45,240
Hutter had earlier defined an agent called ICSI

86
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:48,160
that maximizes exactly this kind of quantity.

87
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:54,540
combining Solomonoff's predictor with planning. Consider every program consistent with what you've

88
00:06:54,540 --> 00:07:00,180
seen, weighted by simplicity, and choose the action with the best expected outcome across

89
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:06,260
all of them. In that precise sense, ICSI is the most intelligent possible agent. It is also

90
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:14,600
uncomputable. Approximations exist. In 2011, a team led by Joel Venice built one and tested it

91
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:19,880
on a set of small problems, the largest aversion of Pac-Man, in which the agent could sense

92
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:21,560
only its immediate surroundings.

93
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:24,460
But there's a known problem with all of this.

94
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:29,860
The answer depends on which universal Turing machine you use to measure program length.

95
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:32,480
Yes, and it is not a technicality.

96
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:38,060
Kalmogorov complexity is only defined up to a constant that depends on that reference machine.

97
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:42,780
In 2015, Jan Laika and Hutter showed how much it matters.

98
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:50,020
of reference machine, Ike-C can behave very badly, and Legge and Hutter's measure becomes,

99
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:56,700
in their word, subjective. Worse, by one natural standard of optimality, every possible policy

100
00:07:56,700 --> 00:08:01,920
counts as optimal across the class of all computable environments, which makes that

101
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:08,960
standard empty. Universal intelligence is universal only relative to a choice of what counts as simple.

102
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:12,960
That connects to one of the deepest results in this area,

103
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:14,920
the no-free-lunch theorems,

104
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:18,000
proved by David Walpert in the mid-1990s

105
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:19,520
for learning on his own

106
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:21,960
and for optimization with William Macready.

107
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:24,640
Averaged over all possible problems,

108
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:27,120
every learning or optimization method

109
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:29,320
performs exactly as well as every other.

110
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:32,180
Averaged across every conceivable world,

111
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:34,260
no method beats random guessing.

112
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:38,620
So there's no such thing as general intelligence in the absolute.

113
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:40,440
Not in the absolute.

114
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:44,140
An intelligence is good across a particular distribution of problems,

115
00:08:44,160 --> 00:08:48,020
and it gets there by having assumptions, priors, that fit that distribution.

116
00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:51,740
Legge and Hutter's simplicity weighting is one such assumption,

117
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:53,660
that the world tends to be simple.

118
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:57,940
Evolution built different assumptions into crows, octopuses, and us,

119
00:08:58,100 --> 00:08:59,440
fitted to our different worlds.

120
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:02,460
So whenever someone calls a system generally intelligent,

121
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:04,600
the right follow-up question is,

122
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:06,080
general across what?

123
00:09:07,220 --> 00:09:13,800
In 2019, Francois Chalet, then at Google, published a long paper called On the Measure

124
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:19,400
of Intelligence that builds on exactly that point. His first move is to separate skill from

125
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:24,820
intelligence. Skill at a task can be bought in two ways, by building in prior knowledge,

126
00:09:24,820 --> 00:09:31,780
or by training on vast amounts of task-specific experience. A chess engine is enormously skilled

127
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:36,840
at chess, and that tells you little about its intelligence. So Chalet defines intelligence

128
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:43,340
as skill acquisition efficiency. How efficiently a system turns its priors and its experience into

129
00:09:43,340 --> 00:09:49,700
skill on new tasks, weighted by how hard those tasks are to generalize to. That has a pointed

130
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:55,660
consequence. Two systems with equal skill can differ greatly in intelligence if one needed

131
00:09:55,660 --> 00:10:01,640
far more data. On Chalet's measure, a system that learned from trillions of words what a child

132
00:10:01,780 --> 00:10:07,520
learns from millions is showing a great deal of skill and not much efficiency. To compare fairly

133
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:12,280
with people, Chalet proposed controlling for priors, and he turned to the developmental

134
00:10:12,280 --> 00:10:18,040
psychologist Elizabeth Spelke's work on core knowledge, a small set of systems that infants

135
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:23,960
seem to have very early, for objects, for agents and their goals, for number, and for geometry.

136
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:30,340
A fair test of general intelligence should require only those priors, plus the ability to learn.

137
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:34,600
the abstraction and reasoning corpus, or ARC.

138
00:10:34,920 --> 00:10:38,640
Each task shows a few examples of colored grids being transformed

139
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:41,160
and asks for the output on a new input.

140
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:44,820
Every task follows a different rule, designed to be new.

141
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:48,400
Most people solve most of them, usually within a few minutes.

142
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:52,840
For years, AI systems did terribly on ARC,

143
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,460
even as they aced standardized exams.

144
00:10:56,300 --> 00:10:59,520
Competitions in 2020 and 2024

145
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:03,700
produced steady progress through program search and test time training,

146
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,000
in which a model is briefly fine-tuned on each task's own examples.

147
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:14,460
Then, in December 2024, a preview of OpenAI's O3 reasoning model

148
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:17,700
scored about 76% on a held-out set,

149
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:22,200
using six samples per task at roughly $26 a task.

150
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:27,880
With over 1,000 samples per task and around 170 times the computing cost,

151
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:32,660
Two caveats belong with those numbers.

152
00:11:33,020 --> 00:11:37,520
The tested model had been trained on three-quarters of ARK's public training set,

153
00:11:37,660 --> 00:11:41,280
and the model OpenAI later released was a different version.

154
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:46,420
A second, harder version, ARKIG i2, launched in March 2025,

155
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:49,920
and pure language models scored essentially zero on it.

156
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:55,240
At the end of that year's competition, the winning OpenEntry, called NVARK,

157
00:11:55,380 --> 00:11:58,780
scored about 24% at around $0.20 a task.

158
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:04,820
The best verified commercial model was Claude Opus 4.5 at about 38%.

159
00:12:04,820 --> 00:12:09,480
That is a model from Anthropic, the company that makes the model writing this series.

160
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:12,900
A refinement harness built by a company called Poetic,

161
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:18,700
wrapped around Google's Gemini 3 Pro, reached 54% at about $30 a task.

162
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:22,760
And the competition's paper award went to a tiny recursive model,

163
00:12:23,060 --> 00:12:25,400
with 7 million parameters and two layers,

164
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:30,420
which scored 45% on the first version of ARC and 8% on the second.

165
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:36,920
7 million parameters, beating several far larger language models from earlier that year.

166
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:39,900
That says something about what scale is buying.

167
00:12:40,220 --> 00:12:44,620
It says that on this narrow kind of task, a small network with the right structure,

168
00:12:44,860 --> 00:12:48,680
trained on the puzzles themselves, can outdo much larger general models.

169
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:53,280
Then came ARC a GI3, launched in March 2026,

170
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:57,500
which abandons static puzzles for interactive games with no instructions.

171
00:12:57,960 --> 00:13:02,000
A system has to explore, work out what the game is, and win,

172
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:04,440
and it is scored on how efficiently it acts.

173
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:07,360
At launch, human testers solved all of the games,

174
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:11,080
and Frontier AI systems scored around half of 1%.

175
00:13:11,080 --> 00:13:14,240
Meanwhile, the older test was nearly saturated.

176
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:18,440
By July, ARC's verified results put Claude Opus 5,

177
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:22,960
another anthropic model, at about 90% on ARC a GI2,

178
00:13:23,280 --> 00:13:25,440
and about 30% on the new games.

179
00:13:25,620 --> 00:13:33,800
In September 2026, OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra scored about 63% under ARK's standard test harness

180
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:38,820
and nearly 100% under a different harness, a so-called provider adapter.

181
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:43,740
Even with the reasoning setting matched, it was about 63 against 99.

182
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:48,320
The ARK prize organizers were explicit that this was not AGI.

183
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:53,080
63 versus 100. Same model, same games.

184
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:59,180
whose intelligence is being measured, the models or the model plus whatever was built around it?

185
00:13:59,440 --> 00:14:02,400
That's exactly the question, and it generalizes.

186
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:09,620
Tools, retries, memory, prompting strategies, and hand-built harnesses can change a score dramatically,

187
00:14:09,660 --> 00:14:12,660
which brings us to the science of measurement itself.

188
00:14:13,920 --> 00:14:19,200
Measuring AI capability has become its own discipline, and it is harder than it looks.

189
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:25,500
Benchmarks saturate, a test that once separated systems ends up with everyone near the ceiling.

190
00:14:26,220 --> 00:14:27,040
Benchmarks leak.

191
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:29,920
Test questions find their way into training data,

192
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:34,200
so models can score well partly by memory, a modern clever hands.

193
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:39,020
When a team led by Hu Zhang wrote a fresh set of grade school math problems

194
00:14:39,020 --> 00:14:40,960
matched to a popular benchmark,

195
00:14:41,460 --> 00:14:44,280
some models' accuracy dropped by up to 8%,

196
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:49,920
and the size of the drop tracked how readily a model could reproduce the original test items.

197
00:14:50,220 --> 00:14:55,760
Many models, especially at the frontier, showed little sign of overfitting, which is worth saying

198
00:14:55,760 --> 00:15:02,040
too. And benchmarks invite optimization, which triggers Goodhart's law in the anthropologist

199
00:15:02,040 --> 00:15:07,660
Marilyn Strathern's well-known phrasing, when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good

200
00:15:07,660 --> 00:15:13,380
measure. There's a deeper problem, one that episode one would recognize, construct validity.

201
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:18,180
In 2025, a team led by Andrew Bean at Oxford

202
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:21,720
reviewed 445 language model benchmarks.

203
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:26,060
Only about 16% used statistical tests to compare models

204
00:15:26,060 --> 00:15:29,220
and roughly half set out to measure an abstract concept,

205
00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:33,060
like reasoning or safety, without clearly defining it.

206
00:15:33,900 --> 00:15:35,600
Psychometrics spent a century learning

207
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:37,060
to ask what a test measures

208
00:15:37,060 --> 00:15:39,900
and whether it measures the same thing across groups.

209
00:15:40,180 --> 00:15:43,580
Much of AI evaluation is starting that work now.

210
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:47,680
Researchers have also tried to define the target itself.

211
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:52,080
One approach defines general intelligence by comparison with people.

212
00:15:52,400 --> 00:15:59,020
In October 2025, 33 researchers led by Dan Hendricks proposed defining AGI

213
00:15:59,020 --> 00:16:04,060
as matching the cognitive versatility and proficiency of a well-educated adult.

214
00:16:04,340 --> 00:16:10,460
Across 10 domains drawn from the cattle horn carol model, we met in episode 1, weighted equally.

215
00:16:10,460 --> 00:16:22,320
They scored GPT-4 at about 27% and GPT-5 at about 57%, with long-term memory storage the weakest area, where both models scored zero.

216
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:30,580
One natural objection is that this measures how human-like a profile of abilities is, which may not be the same thing as intelligence.

217
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:33,580
A different approach measures autonomy directly.

218
00:16:34,120 --> 00:16:39,760
The research group Meter estimates the length of tasks, measured by how long they take skilled people,

219
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:43,640
that AI agents can complete with 50% reliability.

220
00:16:43,980 --> 00:16:50,940
By their estimates, that Horizon doubled roughly every seven months between 2019 and 2025,

221
00:16:51,580 --> 00:16:54,380
and closer to every four months since 2023.

222
00:16:54,880 --> 00:17:00,260
The most capable systems measured in 2026 are put at more than a working day.

223
00:17:00,260 --> 00:17:07,540
But Meter itself warns that measurements above about 16 hours are unreliable with its current set of test tasks.

224
00:17:07,720 --> 00:17:11,980
And a third approach borrows the psychometrics of episode 1 directly.

225
00:17:12,500 --> 00:17:19,200
In 2023, David Elitch analyzed more than 1,200 language models on a public leaderboard

226
00:17:19,200 --> 00:17:24,940
and found that a single factor explained about 85% of the variation in their benchmark scores.

227
00:17:25,160 --> 00:17:27,660
A strong machine, positive manifold.

228
00:17:27,660 --> 00:17:39,500
In April 2026, the complexity scientist David Krakauer asked how that structure changes over time, in a paper titled The Rise and Fall of G in AGI.

229
00:17:39,920 --> 00:17:47,160
Across 39 models, on the eight most widely reported benchmarks, scores correlated strongly, around 0.8 on average.

230
00:17:47,340 --> 00:17:55,240
And on one core battery, the first factor explained about 92% of the variation in 2023 and early 2024.

231
00:17:55,860 --> 00:18:03,760
It had begun to slip by mid-2024, and after reasoning specialized models arrived, it fell to about 64%.

232
00:18:03,760 --> 00:18:11,900
The data are thin. Less than half of the model and benchmark combinations were actually measured, and the latest period has only four models.

233
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:16,960
So treat it as a suggestion. But it fits the common cause story from episode one.

234
00:18:17,180 --> 00:18:20,940
When scale was the main thing changing, everything rose together.

235
00:18:20,940 --> 00:18:27,140
As labs began training for particular skills, the general factor weakened within each new group of models.

236
00:18:27,360 --> 00:18:31,920
Though pooled across all of them, it still accounts for about 90% of the variation.

237
00:18:32,300 --> 00:18:38,800
Which would make machine G an artifact of how the systems were built, not a property of intelligence.

238
00:18:39,260 --> 00:18:42,000
Or a property of one era of building them.

239
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:49,340
Either way, the structure of machine abilities has shifted within a few years, far faster than anything measured in people.

240
00:18:50,940 --> 00:18:53,260
So after all of this, do we have a definition?

241
00:18:53,760 --> 00:18:56,940
We have several, and they disagree in revealing ways.

242
00:18:57,180 --> 00:19:01,960
The psychometric view defines intelligence by how abilities cover in people.

243
00:19:02,200 --> 00:19:09,140
The algorithmic view of Solomonoff, Legge and Hutter defines it as expected success across all environments,

244
00:19:09,500 --> 00:19:15,440
weighted by simplicity, elegant, uncomputable, and relative to a choice of reference machine.

245
00:19:15,960 --> 00:19:19,480
Shale defines it as efficiency of learning relative to priors.

246
00:19:20,940 --> 00:19:27,080
use define it by what systems can actually do, how long they can work on their own, and how human-like

247
00:19:27,080 --> 00:19:33,940
their profile is. For large language models, these come apart sharply. Their huge training sets count

248
00:19:33,940 --> 00:19:40,260
heavily against them on Chalet's measure and not at all on Legg and Hudders. Their uneven profile

249
00:19:40,260 --> 00:19:45,960
shows up immediately on Hendrix's, and their scores depend on harnesses in ways that make every number

250
00:19:45,960 --> 00:19:51,340
contestable. What the definitions share is a core idea that will run through the rest of the series.

251
00:19:51,960 --> 00:19:56,980
Intelligence is bound up with prediction, with building models good enough to anticipate what

252
00:19:56,980 --> 00:20:03,720
comes next. Legg and Hutter have not stopped at intelligence, either. In September 2026,

253
00:20:04,140 --> 00:20:08,960
both were among the authors of a framework for assessing whether AI systems might be conscious,

254
00:20:09,300 --> 00:20:14,760
which we'll examine in episode 12. But where does the drive to predict come from in the first place?

255
00:20:16,020 --> 00:20:21,300
we go back to the origin of life and to the idea that life itself is computation.

